feat(scripts): nxs-restore-drill — read-only encrypted-snapshot drill

Roadmap item 18. Counterpart to scripts/nexora-restore-drill.sh
(which only handles plain .db backups). Verifies an encrypted .nxs
snapshot can actually be decrypted on the customer's machine BEFORE
the disaster — because the encrypted-snapshot path is the one the
customer will actually run on update failure.

scripts/nexora-nxs-restore-drill.ts
  bun run scripts/nexora-nxs-restore-drill.ts <snapshot.nxs>
    [--license-secret=<hex>] [--machine-id=<id>]
    [--extract=<out.tar.gz>]

Behavior:
  - Read-only by default. With --extract, writes plaintext tar.gz
    (which the operator can then expand into a scratch volume to
    practice the full restore).
  - license-secret + machine-id resolve from flag → env → file in
    that order (license.lic, /etc/machine-id, /data/app/machine-id).
  - Pre-checks NXS1 magic before key derivation (cheap fail).
  - Verifies plaintext starts with gzip magic (0x1f 0x8b) — catches
    cross-version drift where the key matches but the format doesn't.

Exit codes:
  0 PASS — decrypts, auth-tag verifies, plaintext is tar.gz
  1 FATAL — args / file missing / required material not found
  2 FAIL — wrong magic / decrypt failed (wrong key / wrong machine
            / tampering)
  3 FAIL — decrypts but plaintext format wrong (cross-version drift)

Why a TS script and not a shell wrapper:
  - The crypto matches backend/src/lib/snapshot-crypto.ts exactly
    by IMPORTING from it. A bash + openssl reimpl would drift the
    moment we tweak HKDF salt/info strings.

Reuses existing lib code — no new prod-path surface, no test churn.
Build still 510 modules. Lib tests 88/88 untouched.